  "narrative": "All twelve discovery tests have adequate cell counts and valid Welch intervals. Because the original claims used direction -1, positive signed estimates correspond to raw stronger dependency and negative signed estimates correspond to the opposite raw direction. I use the absolute outcome mean for the 10% relative-difference threshold, while also checking outcome SD because outcome_014 and outcome_006 have near-zero means. The dominant result is H1: signed estimate -0.796 implies a raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast of about +0.796 for feature_027=1 versus 0 on outcome_014. That is about 8.5 times the absolute outcome mean and about 2.2 SD, far above the clinical threshold, and the interval is entirely against the anticipated negative direction. Multiplicity over twelve tests cannot plausibly explain an effect of this magnitude, so the original directional hypothesis is rejected; however, the reversed difference is strong enough to register as a changed proposal and request independent validation. Most other mean differences are small and either have intervals excluding clinically meaningful hypothesized effects or are too wide/uncertain to support a claim. H2, H9, and H12 remain unresolved because their intervals include clinically meaningful hypothesized effects but the point estimates are small and the tests are not statistically decisive. H3-H5 and H7-H8 are rejected because their hypothesized-direction intervals do not reach the 10% threshold and point estimates are near zero. H6, H10, and H11 are rejected as anticipated directional effects; some of their intervals admit opposite or wide effects, but not enough to justify immediate acceptance or to outweigh multiple-testing and interaction-estimation uncertainty. The results are associations within this dataset only; causal interpretation and generalizability remain open.",
